<subsection xml:id="subsec-double-integrals-evaluation">
  <title>Evaluating Double Integrals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A double integral over an arbitrary planar domain is difficult to evaluate
      directly from its definition.
      We therefore restrict to a family of domains — called <em>normal domains</em>
      — for which the double integral reduces to two successive one-variable
      integrals.
      This section develops the Cartesian (rectangular) and polar evaluation
      formulas and illustrates them with worked examples.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Normal domains ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-normal-domains-2d">
    <title>Normal Domains in <m>\R^2</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>normal domain</term> is a bounded planar region described by
        a pair of inequalities of one of the following two types.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Type I</em> (vertical slices): the <m>x</m>-boundaries are
            constant, and the <m>y</m>-boundaries depend on <m>x</m>:
            <me>
              a \leq x \leq b, \qquad y_l(x) \leq y \leq y_u(x).
            </me>
            Here the subscripts <m>l</m> and <m>u</m> stand for
            <em>lower</em> and <em>upper</em>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Type II</em> (horizontal slices): the <m>y</m>-boundaries are
            constant, and the <m>x</m>-boundaries depend on <m>y</m>:
            <me>
              a \leq y \leq b, \qquad g_l(y) \leq x \leq g_r(y).
            </me>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- Normal domain figure -->
  <figure xml:id="fig-normal-domains-2d">
    <caption>
      Left: normal domain of Type I, bounded by curves <m>y = y_l(x)</m>
      (lower) and <m>y = y_u(x)</m> (upper) between <m>x = a</m> and <m>x = b</m>.
      Right: normal domain of Type II, bounded by curves <m>x = g_l(y)</m>
      (left) and <m>x = g_r(y)</m> (right) between <m>y = a</m> and <m>y = b</m>.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-normal-domains-2d" width="90%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, font=\small]
          %--- Type I (left panel) ---
          \begin{scope}
            \fill[blue!15]
              (0.5,0.4) -- (0.5,1.9)
              .. controls (1.0,2.2) and (1.5,2.3) .. (2.5,2.1)
              -- (2.5,0.6)
              .. controls (1.8,0.2) and (1.0,0.3) .. (0.5,0.4);
            \draw[thick]
              (0.5,0.4)
              .. controls (1.0,0.3) and (1.8,0.2) .. (2.5,0.6)
              node[right] {$y=y_l(x)$};
            \draw[thick]
              (0.5,1.9)
              .. controls (1.0,2.2) and (1.5,2.3) .. (2.5,2.1)
              node[right] {$y=y_u(x)$};
            \draw[dashed] (0.5,0) -- (0.5,1.9);
            \draw[dashed] (2.5,0) -- (2.5,2.1);
            \draw[->,thick] (-0.1,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.1) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
            \node[below] at (0.5,0) {$a$};
            \node[below] at (2.5,0) {$b$};
            \node at (1.5,1.25) {$A$};
            \node[below] at (1.5,-0.5) {Type I};
          \end{scope}
          %--- Type II (right panel) ---
          \begin{scope}[xshift=5.0cm]
            \fill[orange!20]
              (0.3,0.5) -- (1.8,0.5)
              .. controls (2.1,1.0) and (2.2,1.5) .. (1.9,2.5)
              -- (0.5,2.5)
              .. controls (0.1,2.0) and (0.2,1.0) .. (0.3,0.5);
            \draw[thick]
              (0.3,0.5)
              .. controls (0.2,1.0) and (0.1,2.0) .. (0.5,2.5);
            \draw[thick]
              (1.8,0.5)
              .. controls (2.1,1.0) and (2.2,1.5) .. (1.9,2.5)
              ;
            \node[above] at (0.95,2.65) {$x=g_l(y)$};
            \draw[gray,thin] (0.9,2.5) -- (0.5,2.35);
            \node[above] at (2.35,2.65) {$x=g_r(y)$};
            \draw[gray,thin] (2.25,2.5) -- (1.95,2.25);
            \draw[dashed] (0,0.5) -- (0.3,0.5);
            \draw[dashed] (0,2.5) -- (0.5,2.5);
            \draw[->,thick] (-0.1,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.1) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
            \node[left] at (0,0.5) {$a$};
            \node[left] at (0,2.5) {$b$};
            \node at (1.1,1.5) {$A$};
            \node[below] at (1.5,-0.5) {Type II};
          \end{scope}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <!-- ─── Normal domain examples ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-normal-domain-disk">
    <title>Normal Domain Representations</title>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Express the disk of radius 2 centered at <m>(1,0)</m> as a normal
          domain of Type I.</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          The boundary circle satisfies <m>(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 4</m>.
          Solving for <m>y</m> gives the upper and lower boundary curves:
          <me>
            y = \pm\sqrt{4-(x-1)^2}.
          </me>
          The <m>x</m>-extremes of the disk are <m>x = -1</m> and <m>x = 3</m>.
          Therefore the domain is:
          <md>
            <mrow>-1 \amp\leq x \leq 3,</mrow>
            <mrow>-\sqrt{4-(x-1)^2} \amp\leq y \leq \sqrt{4-(x-1)^2}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Express the region <m>A</m> between the parabolas
          <m>y = 2 - 3x^2</m> and <m>y = -x^2</m> as a normal domain.</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          First find the intersection points:
          <me>
            2 - 3x^2 = -x^2 \;\Rightarrow\; 2 = 2x^2 \;\Rightarrow\; x = \pm 1.
          </me>
          For <m>x \in [-1,1]</m> the parabola <m>y = 2-3x^2</m> lies above
          <m>y = -x^2</m>. The normal domain is:
          <md>
            <mrow>-1 \amp\leq x \leq 1,</mrow>
            <mrow>-x^2 \amp\leq y \leq 2 - 3x^2.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Motivation for iterated integrals ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-iterated-integral-derivation">
    <title>Deriving the Iterated Integral (Type I)</title>
    <p>
      For a Type I domain <m>a \leq x \leq b</m>, <m>y_l(x) \leq y \leq y_u(x)</m>,
      the Riemann sum can be organised in two stages:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Inner sum (fix <m>x</m>, sum in <m>y</m>).</em>
          Subdivide <m>A</m> into vertical strips of width <m>\Delta x_j</m>.
          Within the <m>j</m>-th strip, subdivide further into rectangles of
          area <m>\Delta A_{jk} = \Delta x_j \,\Delta y_k</m>.
          The representative point <m>(x_j,y_k)</m> is chosen inside the
          corresponding rectangle, so in particular
          <m>y_l(x_j)\leq y_k\leq y_u(x_j)</m>.
          Summing over all rectangles in this strip approximates the volume
          of the strip:
          <me>
            V_j \approx \sum_k f(x_j, y_k)\,\Delta y_k\cdot\Delta x_j.
          </me>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Outer sum (sum in <m>x</m>).</em>
          Summing over all strips:
          <me>
            \sum_j V_j \;\to\;
            \int_a^b \!\left(\int_{y_l(x)}^{y_u(x)} f(x,y)\,dy\right)dx.
          </me>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <figure xml:id="fig-iterated-integral-type-i-strip">
      <caption>
        A Type I domain is first divided into vertical strips. In one fixed
        strip <m>x\approx x_j</m>, the <m>y</m>-values run from
        <m>y_l(x_j)</m> to <m>y_u(x_j)</m> and are subdivided into small
        intervals of length <m>\Delta y_k</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-iterated-integral-type-i-strip" width="62%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15, font=\small]
            \fill[blue!12]
              (0.45,0.55)
              .. controls (1.2,0.25) and (2.6,0.25) .. (3.6,0.7)
              -- (3.6,2.15)
              .. controls (2.5,2.45) and (1.35,2.35) .. (0.45,1.75)
              -- cycle;
            \draw[thick, blue!65!black]
              (0.45,0.55)
              .. controls (1.2,0.25) and (2.6,0.25) .. (3.6,0.7)
              node[right] {$y=y_l(x)$};
            \draw[thick, blue!65!black]
              (0.45,1.75)
              .. controls (1.35,2.35) and (2.5,2.45) .. (3.6,2.15)
              node[right] {$y=y_u(x)$};
            \draw[dashed] (0.45,0) -- (0.45,1.75);
            \draw[dashed] (3.6,0) -- (3.6,2.15);
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0) -- (4.25,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.1) -- (0,2.85) node[above] {$y$};
            \node[below] at (0.45,0) {$a$};
            \node[below] at (3.6,0) {$b$};
            \fill[red!16, opacity=0.85] (2.0,0.55) rectangle (2.35,2.28);
            \draw[red!65!black, thick] (2.0,0.55) rectangle (2.35,2.28);
            \draw[red!65!black] (2.0,0.95) -- (2.35,0.95);
            \draw[red!65!black] (2.0,1.35) -- (2.35,1.35);
            \draw[red!65!black] (2.0,1.75) -- (2.35,1.75);
            \draw[latex-latex, red!70!black] (2.0,-0.35) -- (2.35,-0.35)
              node[midway, below] {$\Delta x_j$};
            \draw[latex-latex, red!70!black] (2.5,0.95) -- (2.5,1.35)
              node[midway, right] {$\Delta y_k$};
            \fill[red!80!black] (2.18,1.16) circle (1.4pt);
            \node[above right, red!80!black] at (2.18,1.16) {$(x_j,y_k)$};
            \node[below] at (2.18,0) {$x_j$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The inner integral (in <m>y</m>) is computed first, treating <m>x</m> as a
      constant.  Its result depends only on <m>x</m> and is then integrated in the
      outer integral.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Main theorem ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-double-integral-cartesian">
    <title>Double Integral in Cartesian Coordinates</title>
    <statement>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            For a <em>Type I</em> normal domain
            (<m>a \leq x \leq b</m>, <m>y_l(x) \leq y \leq y_u(x)</m>):
            <me>
              \iint\limits_A f(x,y)\,dA
              = \int_a^b \int_{y_l(x)}^{y_u(x)} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.
            </me>
            The inner integral (variable <m>y</m>) is evaluated first,
            with <m>x</m> treated as a parameter.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For a <em>Type II</em> normal domain
            (<m>a \leq y \leq b</m>, <m>g_l(y) \leq x \leq g_r(y)</m>):
            <me>
              \iint\limits_A f(x,y)\,dA
              = \int_a^b \int_{g_l(y)}^{g_r(y)} f(x,y)\,dx\,dy.
            </me>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If <m>f</m> is continuous on a rectangular domain
        <m>[a,b] \times [c,d]</m>, the order of integration may be reversed
        without changing the result:
        <me>
          \int_a^b \int_c^d f(x,y)\,dy\,dx
          =
          \int_c^d \int_a^b f(x,y)\,dx\,dy.
        </me>
        This is the simplest form of <em>Fubini's theorem</em>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Worked examples: Cartesian ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-double-integral-triangle">
    <title>Double Integral over a Triangular Domain</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute
        <me>
          \int_0^1 \int_0^{1-x} (2xy - x^2 - y^2)\,dy\,dx.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The domain is the triangle with vertices <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(1,0)</m>,
        and <m>(0,1)</m>, described as a Type I normal domain with
        <m>0 \leq x \leq 1</m> and <m>0 \leq y \leq 1 - x</m>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-triangle-integration-domain">
        <caption>
          The triangular domain for
          <m>0 \leq x \leq 1</m>, <m>0 \leq y \leq 1-x</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-triangle-integration-domain" width="42%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3.0]
              \fill[blue!18] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (0,1) -- cycle;
              \draw[->,thick] (-0.08,0) -- (1.18,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.08) -- (0,1.18) node[above] {$y$};
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (0,1) -- (1,0)
                node[midway, above right] {$y=1-x$};
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (0,0) -- (1,0);
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (0,0) -- (0,1);
              \draw[dashed,red!70!black] (0.55,0) -- (0.55,0.45);
              \fill[red!70!black] (0.55,0.22) circle (0.7pt);
              \node[below] at (0.55,0) {$x$};
              \node[right,red!70!black] at (0.57,0.25) {\small$0\leq y\leq 1-x$};
              \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
              \node[below] at (1,0) {$1$};
              \node[left] at (0,1) {$1$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Inner integral (in <m>y</m>):</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^{1-x}(2xy - x^2 - y^2)\,dy
            \amp= \left[xy^2 - x^2 y - \tfrac{1}{3}y^3\right]_{y=0}^{y=1-x}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= x(1-x)^2 - x^2(1-x) - \tfrac{1}{3}(1-x)^3.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Expanding and collecting:</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \amp= x(1 - 2x + x^2) - x^2 + x^3 - \tfrac{1}{3}(1 - 3x + 3x^2 - x^3)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= x - 2x^2 + x^3 - x^2 + x^3
                 - \tfrac{1}{3} + x - x^2 + \tfrac{1}{3}x^3
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \tfrac{7}{3}x^3 - 4x^2 + 2x - \tfrac{1}{3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Outer integral (in <m>x</m>):</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^1\!\left(\tfrac{7}{3}x^3 - 4x^2 + 2x - \tfrac{1}{3}\right)dx
            \amp= \left[\tfrac{7}{12}x^4 - \tfrac{4}{3}x^3 + x^2 - \tfrac{1}{3}x\right]_0^1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \tfrac{7}{12} - \tfrac{16}{12} + \tfrac{12}{12} - \tfrac{4}{12}
             = -\tfrac{1}{12}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-geometric-center-parabola">
    <title>Geometric Center of a Parabolic Region</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the geometric center of the region <m>A</m> bounded by
        <m>y = 2 - 3x^2</m> and <m>y = -x^2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        From <xref ref="ex-normal-domain-disk"/>, the domain is
        <m>-1 \leq x \leq 1</m>, <m>-x^2 \leq y \leq 2 - 3x^2</m>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-parabolic-centroid-domain">
        <caption>
          The parabolic region between <m>y=-x^2</m> and
          <m>y=2-3x^2</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-parabolic-centroid-domain" width="52%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.65]
              \fill[blue!18,domain=-1:1,smooth,variable=\x]
                plot ({\x},{2-3*\x*\x})
                -- plot[domain=1:-1] ({\x},{-\x*\x})
                -- cycle;
              \draw[->,thick] (-1.35,0) -- (1.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,-1.25) -- (0,2.25) node[above] {$y$};
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=-1.1:1.1,smooth,variable=\x]
                plot ({\x},{2-3*\x*\x}) node[right] {$y=2-3x^2$};
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=-1.1:1.1,smooth,variable=\x]
                plot ({\x},{-\x*\x}) node[right] {$y=-x^2$};
              \draw[dashed,gray] (-1,-1) -- (-1,0);
              \draw[dashed,gray] (1,-1) -- (1,0);
              \fill[red!75!black] (0,0.6) circle (1.5pt);
              \node[right,red!75!black] at (0.03,0.6) {$C=(0,\tfrac35)$};
              \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
              \node[below] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
              \node[below] at (1,0) {$1$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        By symmetry, the centroid lies on the <m>y</m>-axis, so <m>x_C = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Area:</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            F(A) \amp= \int_{-1}^1 \int_{-x^2}^{2-3x^2} 1\,dy\,dx
            = \int_{-1}^1 \bigl[(2-3x^2) - (-x^2)\bigr]\,dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \int_{-1}^1 (2 - 2x^2)\,dx
            = \left[2x - \tfrac{2}{3}x^3\right]_{-1}^1 = \tfrac{8}{3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em><m>y</m>-coordinate of the centroid:</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            y_C \amp= \frac{1}{F(A)}\int_{-1}^1\int_{-x^2}^{2-3x^2} y\,dy\,dx
            = \frac{3}{8}\int_{-1}^1
              \left[\frac{y^2}{2}\right]_{y=-x^2}^{y=2-3x^2}dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \frac{3}{8}\int_{-1}^1
              \frac{(2-3x^2)^2 - x^4}{2}\,dx
            = \frac{3}{8}\int_{-1}^1 \left(2 - 6x^2 + 4x^4\right)\,dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \frac{3}{4}\int_0^1 \left(2 - 6x^2 + 4x^4\right)\,dx
            = \frac{3}{4}\left[2x - 2x^3 + \frac{4}{5}x^5\right]_0^1
            = \frac{3}{5}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The geometric center is <m>\bigl(0,\,\tfrac{3}{5}\bigr)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Polar coordinates ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-polar-domain">
    <title>Domains in Polar Coordinates</title>
    <p>
      For domains with circular or radial symmetry it is convenient to use
      <em>polar coordinates</em> <m>(r, \varphi)</m>, where
      <m>x = r\cos\varphi</m> and <m>y = r\sin\varphi</m>.
      A polar normal domain is described by:
      <me>
        r_i(\varphi) \leq r \leq r_a(\varphi),
        \qquad
        \varphi_1 \leq \varphi \leq \varphi_2.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The key point is that the <em>area element</em> in polar coordinates is
      <em>not</em> simply <m>dr\,d\varphi</m>.
      A small polar patch bounded by radii <m>r</m> and <m>r + dr</m> and
      angles <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\varphi + d\varphi</m> has area
      <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\varphi</m>
      (the arc length <m>r\,d\varphi</m> times the radial thickness <m>dr</m>).
      The extra factor <m>r</m> — called the <em>Jacobian</em> — must always
      appear when changing to polar coordinates.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-polar-area-element">
      <caption>
        The polar area element <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\varphi</m>.
        The patch is bounded by the circles <m>r</m> and <m>r + dr</m>
        and the rays <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\varphi + d\varphi</m>.
        Its arc length is <m>r\,d\varphi</m> and its radial thickness is
        <m>dr</m>, giving area <m>r\,dr\,d\varphi</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-polar-area-element" width="52%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.4, font=\small]
            % Origin
            \coordinate (O) at (0,0);
            % Angles (in degrees)
            \def\phiOne{25}
            \def\phiTwo{55}
            % Radii
            \def\rInner{1.25}
            \def\rOuter{1.85}
            \tikzset{labelbox/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.9,
              text opacity=1, inner sep=1.2pt}}
            % Shaded wedge patch
            \fill[blue!20]
              (\phiOne:\rInner)
              arc[start angle=\phiOne, end angle=\phiTwo, radius=\rInner]
              --
              (\phiTwo:\rOuter)
              arc[start angle=\phiTwo, end angle=\phiOne, radius=\rOuter]
              -- cycle;
            \draw[thick]
              (\phiOne:\rInner)
              arc[start angle=\phiOne, end angle=\phiTwo, radius=\rInner];
            \draw[thick]
              (\phiOne:\rOuter)
              arc[start angle=\phiOne, end angle=\phiTwo, radius=\rOuter];
            \draw[thick] (\phiOne:\rInner) -- (\phiOne:\rOuter);
            \draw[thick] (\phiTwo:\rInner) -- (\phiTwo:\rOuter);
            % Axes
            \draw[->,thick] (-0.2,0) -- (2.45,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.55) -- (0,2.25) node[above] {$y$};
            % Angle labels
            \draw[->,gray] (0.42,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=\phiOne, radius=0.42];
            \node[labelbox] at (14:0.62) {$\varphi$};
            \draw[->,gray] (0.72,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=\phiTwo, radius=0.72];
            \node[labelbox, anchor=west] at (62:0.82) {$\varphi{+}d\varphi$};
            % Radius labels
            \node[labelbox, below right] at (\phiOne:0.92) {$r$};
            \node[labelbox, below right] at (\phiOne:2.02) {$r{+}dr$};
            % Side-length labels for the small patch
            \draw[latex-latex,blue!70!black] (\phiTwo:\rInner) -- (\phiTwo:\rOuter)
              node[midway,above right,labelbox] {$dr$};
            \draw[latex-latex,blue!70!black]
              (\phiOne:2.08) arc[start angle=\phiOne,end angle=\phiTwo,radius=2.08]
              node[midway,above,labelbox] {$r\,d\varphi$};
            % Area label
            \node[labelbox] at (40:1.55) {$dA$};
            % dA = r dr dphi label
            \node[align=center,labelbox] at (1.55, -0.42)
              {$dA = r\,dr\,d\varphi$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-double-integral-polar">
    <title>Double Integrals in Polar Coordinates</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a polar normal domain
        <m>r_i(\varphi) \leq r \leq r_a(\varphi)</m>,
        <m>\varphi_1 \leq \varphi \leq \varphi_2</m>:
        <me>
          \iint\limits_A f(x,y)\,dA
          = \int_{\varphi_1}^{\varphi_2}
            \int_{r_i(\varphi)}^{r_a(\varphi)}
            f\bigl(r\cos\varphi,\,r\sin\varphi\bigr)\cdot r\,dr\,d\varphi.
        </me>
        The factor <m>r</m> in the integrand is the Jacobian of the coordinate
        change. As in Cartesian coordinates, the inner integral is evaluated
        first with the outer variable held fixed.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Polar examples ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-semidisk-polar">
    <title>Area and Geometric Center of a Semi-Disk</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the area and geometric center of the upper half-disk of radius <m>R</m>
        (the part of the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \leq R^2</m> with <m>y \geq 0</m>).
      </p>
      <p>
        In polar coordinates the domain is <m>0 \leq r \leq R</m>,
        <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq \pi</m>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-semidisk-polar-domain">
        <caption>
          The upper half-disk, described by
          <m>0 \leq r \leq R</m>, <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq \pi</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-semidisk-polar-domain" width="42%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.35]
              \def\R{1.8}
              \fill[blue!18] (-\R,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,radius=\R] -- cycle;
              \draw[->,thick] (-2.15,0) -- (2.15,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.25) -- (0,2.15) node[above] {$y$};
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (-\R,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,radius=\R];
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (-\R,0) -- (\R,0);
              \draw[->,red!70!black] (0,0) -- (50:\R) node[midway,above left] {$R$};
              \draw[->,gray] (0.55,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=125,radius=0.55];
              \node at (0.0,0.72) {\small$0\leq\varphi\leq\pi$};
              \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Area:</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            F(D) \amp= \int_0^{\pi}\int_0^R r\,dr\,d\varphi
            = \int_0^{\pi}\left[\tfrac{1}{2}r^2\right]_0^R d\varphi
            = \tfrac{1}{2}R^2\pi.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Geometric center:</em>
        By symmetry, <m>x_C = 0</m>.
        For <m>y_C</m>, note that <m>y = r\sin\varphi</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \iint_D y\,dA
            \amp= \int_0^{\pi}\int_0^R r\sin\varphi\cdot r\,dr\,d\varphi
            = \int_0^{\pi}\sin\varphi\,d\varphi\cdot\int_0^R r^2\,dr
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \bigl[-\cos\varphi\bigr]_0^{\pi}\cdot\tfrac{R^3}{3}
            = 2\cdot\tfrac{R^3}{3} = \tfrac{2R^3}{3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore
        <me>
          y_C = \frac{1}{F(D)}\iint_D y\,dA
              = \frac{1}{\pi R^2/2}\cdot\frac{2R^3}{3}
              = \frac{4R}{3\pi}.
        </me>
        The geometric center is at <m>\bigl(0,\,\tfrac{4R}{3\pi}\bigr)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-surface-charge-polar">
    <title>Surface Charge via Polar Coordinates</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A point electric charge <m>q</m> is located at height <m>h</m> above the
        origin of a grounded conducting plane (the <m>xy</m>-plane).
        The method of images replaces the grounded plane by a mirror charge
        chosen so that the plane has zero electric potential. For this setup,
        the induced surface charge density is
        <me>
          \sigma(x,y) = -\frac{hq}{2\pi}\cdot
          \frac{1}{\bigl(x^2+y^2+h^2\bigr)^{3/2}}.
        </me>
        Compute the total induced charge on the disk of radius <m>R</m> centered
        at the origin.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-surface-charge-disk-domain">
        <caption>
          The disk on the grounded plane where the induced charge is integrated.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-surface-charge-disk-domain" width="42%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.25]
              \def\R{1.55}
              \fill[blue!18] (0,0) circle (\R);
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (0,0) circle (\R);
              \draw[->,thick] (-2,0) -- (2,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,-2) -- (0,2) node[above] {$y$};
              \draw[->,red!70!black] (0,0) -- (35:\R) node[midway,above] {$R$};
              \node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
              \node at (0,-1.9) {$0\leq r\leq R,\quad 0\leq\varphi\leq2\pi$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        In polar coordinates the disk is <m>0 \leq r \leq R</m>,
        <m>0 \leq \varphi \leq 2\pi</m>, and
        <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            q_D \amp= \iint_D \sigma\,dA
            = -\frac{hq}{2\pi}
              \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^R \frac{r}{\bigl(r^2+h^2\bigr)^{3/2}}\,dr\,d\varphi
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= -hq\int_0^R \frac{r}{\bigl(r^2+h^2\bigr)^{3/2}}\,dr.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Using the substitution <m>u = r^2 + h^2</m>, <m>du = 2r\,dr</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_0^R \frac{r\,dr}{(r^2+h^2)^{3/2}}
            \amp= \frac{1}{2}\int_{h^2}^{R^2+h^2} u^{-3/2}\,du
            = \frac{1}{2}\left[-\frac{2}{\sqrt{u}}\right]_{h^2}^{R^2+h^2}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \frac{1}{h} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{R^2+h^2}}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore
        <me>
          q_D = -hq\left(\frac{1}{h} - \frac{1}{\sqrt{R^2+h^2}}\right)
              = q\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + R^2/h^2}} - 1\right).
        </me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-annular-sector">
    <title>Area of an Annular Sector</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sketch the domain
        <m>D = \{(r,\varphi) \mid 1 \leq r \leq 2,\;
        \pi/6 \leq \varphi \leq \pi\}</m>
        and compute its area.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-annular-sector-domain">
        <caption>
          The annular sector between <m>r=1</m> and <m>r=2</m>, from
          <m>\varphi=\pi/6</m> to <m>\varphi=\pi</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-annular-sector-domain" width="46%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15]
              \fill[blue!18]
                (30:1) arc[start angle=30,end angle=180,radius=1]
                -- (180:2) arc[start angle=180,end angle=30,radius=2]
                -- cycle;
              \draw[->,thick] (-2.35,0) -- (2.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.25) -- (0,2.35) node[above] {$y$};
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (30:1) arc[start angle=30,end angle=180,radius=1];
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (30:2) arc[start angle=30,end angle=180,radius=2];
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (30:1) -- (30:2);
              \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (180:1) -- (180:2);
              \draw[->,gray] (0.55,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=30,radius=0.55];
              \node at (0.8,0.2) {\small$\pi/6$};
              \node[left] at (-2,0) {$r=2$};
              \node[above left] at (120:1) {$r=1$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The domain <m>D</m> is a sector of the annulus between the circles
        <m>r = 1</m> and <m>r = 2</m>, spanning the angle range from
        <m>\pi/6</m> to <m>\pi</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Area:</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            F(D) \amp= \int_{\pi/6}^{\pi}\int_1^2 r\,dr\,d\varphi
            = \int_{\pi/6}^{\pi}\left[\tfrac{r^2}{2}\right]_1^2 d\varphi
            = \int_{\pi/6}^{\pi}\tfrac{3}{2}\,d\varphi
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \frac{3}{2}\!\left(\pi - \frac{\pi}{6}\right)
            = \frac{3}{2}\cdot\frac{5\pi}{6}
            = \frac{5\pi}{4}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-double-integrals-evaluation-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-double-integral-polar-setup">
      <title>Polar Setup for the Unit Disk</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which polar-coordinate description is correct for the unit disk?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>0\le r\le 1,\; 0\le \varphi\le 2\pi</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. This covers the full disk exactly once.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>0\le r\le 2\pi,\; 0\le \varphi\le 1</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The ranges for radius and angle are swapped.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>-1\le r\le 1,\; 0\le \varphi\le \pi</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Standard polar coordinates use nonnegative radii.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>0\le r\le 1,\; 0\le \varphi\le \pi</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. That would cover only a half-disk.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-double-integral-triangle-area">
      <title>Area of a Triangle by Iterated Integration</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute
          <me>
            \int_0^1\int_0^x 1\,dy\,dx.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First integrate in <m>y</m>:
          <me>
            \int_0^x 1\,dy = x.
          </me>
          Then
          <me>
            \int_0^1 x\,dx = \left[\frac{x^2}{2}\right]_0^1 = \frac12.
          </me>
          So the integral equals <m>\tfrac12</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
